# IDE
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# Claude Code local agent/tool settings — machine-specific (e.g. settings.local.json can carry
# local paths or permission grants) and not part of the project.
#
# `.claude/*` (contents) rather than `.claude/` (the directory itself) is deliberate: git cannot
# re-include anything inside an EXCLUDED directory, so a blanket `.claude/` would make the negation
# below impossible. `.claude/skills/` holds the Spec Kit skill definitions, which ARE project files
# — 15 of them are already tracked, and the whole spec-driven workflow depends on them being shared
# rather than local. Under a blanket ignore those 15 would stay tracked (gitignore never untracks)
# but any NEW or renamed skill would silently vanish from `git status` and never get committed —
# exactly the kind of invisible drift this repo cannot afford across two machines.
.claude/*
!.claude/skills/

# Misc
*.log

# Supabase CLI link state (project ref + local temp), never committed
supabase/.temp/
# Local-stack scratch state written by `supabase start`/`db reset` — machine-local, not the
# project's. Appeared the first time anyone dry-ran a migration locally (2026-08-10); ignoring it
# rather than committing it keeps `git status` honest after every local run.
supabase/.branches/

# Secrets — API keys and private keys, never committed.
#
# There is a concrete, documented path into this trap: the probe scripts
# (supabase/scripts/probe-time-parsing.ts, probe-task-refs.ts) read GEMINI_API_KEY from the
# environment, and on Windows the supply route is a `supabase/.env` file read via
# `deno run --env-file=supabase/.env ...` — PowerShell has no inline `VAR=x cmd` form, so the
# bash one-liner those scripts' own comments suggest does not work here. Running edge functions
# locally (`supabase functions serve`) wants the same file. Without these lines, one `git add .`
# after creating it puts a live Gemini key on GitHub.
#
# Nothing of the sort has ever been committed (verified against `git ls-files`, 2026-08-07) —
# this is the safety net, not a cleanup. Note gitignore never untracks: if a secret DOES get
# committed, adding a pattern here hides it from `git status` while leaving it in history, so
# the key must be rotated, not just removed.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# MSYS/Git-Bash crash dumps
sh.exe.stackdump
